// ============================================================================
// Detection: Unexpected East-West (Pod-to-Pod) Network Flow
// MITRE ATT&CK: T1046 - Network Service Discovery
// Source: Container Network Logs (ACNS / Hubble)  [TEMPLATE — see notes below]
// ============================================================================
//
// STATUS: TEMPLATE / UNVERIFIED. This is the network-plane complement to
// lateral-movement.kql (which is audit/token-based). It requires the pod-to-pod
// flow-log plane, which is NOT enabled by default.
//
// ENABLEMENT (two steps):
//   1. ACNS observability — set in infrastructure/modules/aks_cluster.bicep
//      (networkProfile.advancedNetworking.observability). This turns on Hubble.
//   2. Stored "Container Network Logs" → Log Analytics — a SEPARATE, newer AKS
//      feature (flow logs land in a resource-specific table, queryable with KQL).
//      Its enablement path and exact TABLE NAME + SCHEMA are evolving — CONFIRM
//      against current AKS docs and a captured sample before trusting this query.
//      The table below is a placeholder; adjust field names to the real schema.
//
// Once real flow-log data exists, capture a sample and tune this exactly the way
// the audit detections were tuned — the field paths here are best-effort, not verified.
//
// Idea: an attacker moving laterally opens NEW cross-namespace pod-to-pod flows —
// connections between namespaces that normally never talk. Baseline the legitimate
// pairs first, then alert on the rest.

// HOW THIS DETECTION WORKS (once the flow-log plane exists)
// ---------------------------------------------------------
// Unlike the other detections, this reads the NETWORK plane (pod-to-pod flow logs
// from Cilium/Hubble), not the API audit log. The idea: an attacker mapping the
// cluster or pivoting between workloads opens NEW east-west connections between
// namespaces that normally never talk (T1046 Network Service Discovery). We keep
// only cross-namespace, successfully-forwarded flows and roll them up per pod-pair.
//
// TABLE: RetinaNetworkFlowLogs — PLACEHOLDER name; confirm against the real
//        Container Network Logs schema before trusting this.

// CONFIRM the table name once Container Network Logs are enabled (placeholder):
RetinaNetworkFlowLogs
| where TimeGenerated > ago(1h)        // recent window first
| where FlowType == "L3_L4"            // adjust to real schema — keep network-layer connection flows
| where Verdict == "FORWARDED"         // only flows the network policy ALLOWED (a real connection happened)
| where SourceNamespace != DestinationNamespace          // cross-namespace = east-west
| where SourceNamespace !in ("kube-system", "gatekeeper-system")   // baseline / allowlist
// summarize = aggregate. Per (source pod, dest pod, 5-min bucket) count the flows
// and collect the DISTINCT destination ports touched (make_set) — a pod hitting
// many ports on a peer looks like scanning/discovery.
| summarize Flows = count(), Ports = make_set(DestinationPort)
        by SourceNamespace, SourcePod = SourcePodName,
           DestinationNamespace, DestinationPod = DestinationPodName, bin(TimeGenerated, 5m)  // bin() = fixed 5-min time buckets
| extend MitreTechnique = "T1046"    // tag with the ATT&CK technique

variables.$fxv#3:
// ============================================================================
// Detection: hostPath Volume Mount (Container Breakout Vector)
// MITRE ATT&CK: T1611 - Escape to Host
// Source: AKSAuditAdmin
// ============================================================================

// HOW THIS DETECTION WORKS
// -------------------------
// A `hostPath` volume mounts a directory from the NODE's filesystem into the pod.
// Mount something sensitive (/ , /var/run/docker.sock, /etc/kubernetes) and the
// container has a window onto the host — the classic container-breakout primitive
// (T1611 Escape to Host). This detection fires at ADMISSION time, when the pod is
// created, so you catch it before the workload even runs.
//
// The interesting mechanic is mv-apply: a pod can declare MANY volumes, so we need
// to inspect the spec.volumes ARRAY element-by-element and keep only the pods that
// have at least one hostPath entry — reporting exactly which volume and host path.
//
// TABLE: AKSAuditAdmin — write/mutating audit events (pod `create` lands here).

AKSAuditAdmin
| where TimeGenerated > ago(1h)                                  // recent window first
| where Verb == "create" and ObjectRef.resource == "pods"       // only pod-creation events
// mv-apply expands the spec.volumes array into one virtual row per volume, runs
// the inner pipeline on each, and keeps only rows that survive it. The inner
// `where isnotempty(volume.hostPath)` drops every non-hostPath volume, so a pod
// with zero hostPath volumes produces no rows (and drops out of the results).
| mv-apply volume = RequestObject.spec.volumes on (
    where isnotempty(volume.hostPath)                                          // keep hostPath volumes only
    | project VolumeName = tostring(volume.name), HostPath = tostring(volume.hostPath.path)  // surface the volume + host dir
  )
// Tune: CSI node drivers / monitoring agents legitimately mount hostPath in
// kube-system — allowlist those, alert on hostPath in workload namespaces.
| where User.username != "aksService" and User.username !startswith "system:"  // drop AKS + control-plane creators
| project TimeGenerated, User = tostring(User.username), ClientIp = tostring(SourceIps[0]),   // who + from where
          Namespace = tostring(ObjectRef.namespace), Pod = tostring(ObjectRef.name),           // which pod
          VolumeName, HostPath, RequestUri                                                     // and exactly what it mounted
| extend MitreTechnique = "T1611"    // tag with the ATT&CK technique

variables.$fxv#5:
// ============================================================================
// Detection: ClusterRole Granting nodes/proxy (Kubelet API Access)
// MITRE ATT&CK: T1611 - Escape to Host
// Source: AKSAuditAdmin
// ============================================================================

// HOW THIS DETECTION WORKS
// -------------------------
// The nodes/proxy RBAC verb lets a subject talk to the KUBELET API on each node
// directly — which allows executing commands in ANY pod on that node, i.e. a path
// to the host / privilege escalation (T1611 Escape to Host).
//
// DETECTION-DESIGN INSIGHT: you CANNOT reliably catch the abuse at call time,
// because the proxied request goes to the kubelet and does not appear as a
// nodes/proxy call in the API-server audit log. So we detect the ENABLER instead:
// the creation of a ClusterRole whose rules grant `nodes/proxy`. That RBAC write
// is durable and always audited.
//
// A ClusterRole has an ARRAY of rules, each with a `resources` list, so we use
// mv-apply to inspect every rule and keep roles where any rule lists nodes/proxy.
//
// TABLE: AKSAuditAdmin — write/mutating audit events (ClusterRole `create`).

AKSAuditAdmin
| where TimeGenerated > ago(1h)                                  // recent window first
| where Verb == "create" and ObjectRef.resource == "clusterroles"  // only ClusterRole creations
// Tune: the built-in system:prometheus role also grants nodes/proxy, created by aksService.
| where User.username != "aksService" and User.username !startswith "system:"  // drop AKS + control-plane creators
// mv-apply expands the rules[] array to one row per rule; the inner where keeps
// only rules whose `resources` list contains "nodes/proxy" (set_has_element =
// membership test on an array). Rules without it are dropped; a role with no
// matching rule yields no rows and falls out.
| mv-apply rule = RequestObject.rules on (
    where set_has_element(rule.resources, "nodes/proxy")   // wildcard "*" roles are caught by privileged-RBAC detections
    | project Resources = tostring(rule.resources), Verbs = tostring(rule.verbs)  // surface what the rule grants
  )
| project TimeGenerated, User = tostring(User.username), ClientIp = tostring(SourceIps[0]),   // who + from where
          ClusterRole = tostring(ObjectRef.name), Resources, Verbs, RequestUri                 // which role + the offending grant
| extend MitreTechnique = "T1611"    // tag with the ATT&CK technique

variables.$fxv#7:
// ============================================================================
// Detection: Cluster-Wide Secret Dump
// MITRE ATT&CK: T1552.007 - Unsecured Credentials: Container API
// Source: AKSAudit
// ============================================================================

// HOW THIS DETECTION WORKS
// -------------------------
// To grab every Secret in the cluster at once, an attacker issues ONE `list`
// against the cluster-scoped path /api/v1/secrets — the API server returns Secrets
// from ALL namespaces in a single response (T1552.007, credentials via the
// container/K8s API).
//
// KEY MODELLING INSIGHT: because it's a single list, "count how many secret reads
// per user" is the WRONG detection model — there's only one call. The tell is the
// cluster-scoped URI itself. Compare:
//   - cluster-wide (this):     /api/v1/secrets
//   - namespaced (normal app): /api/v1/namespaces/<ns>/secrets
//
// TABLE: AKSAudit — the FULL kube audit log including reads (get/list/watch).
//        This must NOT use AKSAuditAdmin, which drops non-mutating reads and so
//        would never see a `list`.

AKSAudit
| where TimeGenerated > ago(1h)                                  // recent window first
| where ObjectRef.resource == "secrets" and Verb == "list"       // only bulk Secret reads
| where RequestUri startswith "/api/v1/secrets"   // cluster-wide, all namespaces
// Tune: controllers (generic-garbage-collector, resourcequota, namespace-controller)
// list secrets cluster-wide as `system:*` — allowlist those, alert on humans/app SAs.
| where User.username != "aksService" and User.username !startswith "system:"  // drop AKS + control-plane
| project TimeGenerated, User = tostring(User.username), ClientIp = tostring(SourceIps[0]), RequestUri  // who + from where + exact path
| extend MitreTechnique = "T1552.007"    // tag with the ATT&CK sub-technique

variables.$fxv#9:
// ============================================================================
// Detection: Long-Lived Service Account Token Minted
// MITRE ATT&CK: T1098 - Account Manipulation
// Source: AKSAuditAdmin
// ============================================================================

// HOW THIS DETECTION WORKS
// -------------------------
// The TokenRequest API (POST serviceaccounts/<name>/token) issues a bearer token
// for a ServiceAccount on demand. An attacker who can call it against a PRIVILEGED
// existing SA (e.g. kube-system's clusterrole-aggregation-controller) walks away
// with that SA's cluster rights — persistence / credential creation, T1098.
//
// DISAMBIGUATION: nodes-proxy.kql's attack ALSO ends in a TokenRequest. Tell the
// two apart by the TARGET ServiceAccount:
//   - an EXISTING privileged kube-system SA -> this detection
//   - a fresh, purpose-built SA the attacker just created -> nodes-proxy
//
// TABLE: AKSAuditAdmin — write/mutating audit events only (TokenRequest is a
//        `create`, so it lands here rather than in the full-read AKSAudit table).

AKSAuditAdmin
| where TimeGenerated > ago(1h)                          // recent window first
// Match exactly the TokenRequest call: create + serviceaccounts resource + the
// `token` subresource. All three conditions pin it to token minting, nothing else.
| where Verb == "create" and ObjectRef.resource == "serviceaccounts" and ObjectRef.subresource == "token"
// Tune: kubelets (system:node:*) mint projected tokens constantly — allowlist them.
| where User.username != "aksService" and User.username !startswith "system:"  // drop AKS + control-plane callers
| project TimeGenerated, User = tostring(User.username), ClientIp = tostring(SourceIps[0]),   // who + from where
          Namespace = tostring(ObjectRef.namespace), ServiceAccount = tostring(ObjectRef.name), RequestUri  // which SA got a token
| extend MitreTechnique = "T1098"    // tag with the ATT&CK technique
